@extends('layouts/marketing')

<script server>
useSeoMeta({
  title: 'loghq vs Honeybadger - loghq',
  description: 'How loghq, an open-source error tracker you self-host on Postgres, compares to Honeybadger’s hosted monitoring.',
  ogImage: 'https://loghq.org/og.png',
})
useHead({ link: [{ rel: 'canonical', href: 'https://loghq.org/compare/honeybadger' }] })

// Breadcrumb trail. One array drives two consumers — the rendered list in
// the Breadcrumbs partial and the BreadcrumbList JSON-LD below — so the
// markup and the structured data cannot disagree. The last crumb has no
// href or url: it is this page.
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/', url: 'https://loghq.org/' },
  { label: 'Compare', href: '/compare', url: 'https://loghq.org/compare' },
  { label: 'Honeybadger' },
]
</script>

@push('styles')
<style>
  /* Highlight the loghq column (2nd of every 3 grid cells) so the table reads
    at a glance. Uses the same accent-soft token as the rest of the site. */
  .cmp-hl .cmp-cell:nth-child(3n+2) { background: var(--accent-soft); }
  .cmp-note { margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--text-3); font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.55; max-width: 68ch; }
</style>
@endpush

@section('content')
@structuredData({
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: crumbs.map((c, i) => ({ '@type': 'ListItem', position: i + 1, name: c.label, item: c.url })),
})

@include('Breadcrumbs')

<section class="feat-hero shell">
  <span class="kicker">Compare</span>
  <h1>loghq vs Honeybadger</h1>
  <p>Honeybadger is a developer-friendly SaaS that pairs error tracking with uptime and cron monitoring under simple, flat pricing. It is closed source and hosted only. loghq focuses on the error side and gives you an open, self-hostable core, so pick loghq when data ownership matters more than the bundled uptime and cron checks.</p>
  <div class="hero-actions">
    <a class="btn primary" href="/register">Get started</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="/compare">All alternatives</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="reveal section shell" aria-labelledby="table-heading">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="table-heading" class="section-title">loghq and Honeybadger, side by side</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="cmp cmp-hl">
  <div class="cmp-cell">Capability</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">loghq</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Honeybadger</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">What it is</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Focused error and crash tracking</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Errors, uptime and cron monitoring</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Open source</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Yes, open source</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">No</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Self-host / data ownership</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Yes, your own Postgres</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">No, SaaS only</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Pricing model</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Free self-hosted, usage-based hosted</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Flat tiered plans</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Error grouping</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Automatic fingerprinting</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Yes</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Source maps and stack traces</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Full source-mapped traces</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Yes</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Releases and environments</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Built in</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Yes</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Alerting</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">New and spiking issues</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Yes</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-dim">Data retention</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell cmp-ghost">Yours to set when self-hosted</div>
  <div class="cmp-cell">Plan-based</div>
  </div>
  <p class="cmp-note">Where Honeybadger still leads: uptime checks and cron/heartbeat monitoring are built in, so three kinds of monitoring live behind one bill.</p>
</section>

<section class="reveal section shell" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="why-heading" class="section-title">Why teams pick loghq</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-step">
      <span class="flow-num">01</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Own your data</h3>
        <p>Honeybadger is hosted only. loghq self-hosts on your own Postgres, so error data stays inside your infrastructure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-step">
      <span class="flow-num">02</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Open source</h3>
        <p>loghq is open, auditable, and extensible, where Honeybadger ships as a closed product.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-step">
      <span class="flow-num">03</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Just error tracking</h3>
        <p>If you already run your own uptime and cron checks, loghq gives you the error piece without paying for a bundle you will not use.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="reveal section shell" aria-label="Fair comparison">
  <div class="chips">
    <div class="chip">
      <h3>Credit where it is due</h3>
      <p>Honeybadger’s flat pricing and bundled uptime and cron monitoring are genuinely convenient for small teams. loghq trades that bundle for openness and self-hosting.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="chip">
      <h3>What loghq is</h3>
      <p>Open-source error and crash tracking you can run on your own Postgres, or use on a hosted tier. Automatic capture, fingerprint grouping, source-mapped traces, releases, and alerts, without the rest of an observability suite.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="reveal section shell" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="related-heading" class="section-title">More comparisons</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="related">
